# The test pyramid in Tutors

### What each `tests/` folder is for

Tutors Ladder, rung 5 (Proven), talk 1

---

## Why a pyramid

- Many small, fast tests at the bottom: pure functions, one module at a time
- Fewer, slower tests in the middle: a feature through its public functions
- A few slow tests at the top: the built app in a browser, against real services
- Each layer answers a different question. None replaces another.

Rung 5 is the first rung where the tests are the product. Nothing changes for the user.

---

## Upstream's map

`tests/TESTING.md` in tutors-mono-repo maps each folder to a tier, a runner and a command.

| Tier | Folder | Runner |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | `architecture/` | Vitest + `pnpm check:knip` |
| B | `unit/`, `fuzz/` | Vitest (`vitest.config.fuzz.ts` for fuzz) |
| C | `generator/` | Vitest + the Deno generator |
| D | `bdd/` | Vitest + vitest-cucumber |
| G, F | `e2e-stack/` | Playwright against built images |
| J to O | `conformance/`, `observability/`, `performance/`, `security/`, `completeness/`, `suite-health/` | Vitest |

Plus `contract/`, `data/`, `mutation/`, `release/` and `support/`.

---

## The bottom: unit and fuzz (tier B)

- `tests/unit/`: 22 folders, 136 files, grouped by package: `model`, `gen`, `time`, `rbac`, ...
- `tests/fuzz/`: 6 property suites with fast-check, against the real code
- Courses come from one shared arbitrary: `tests/support/arbitraries/course-tree.ts`
- A failure prints `{ seed, path }`; `FUZZ_SEED=... FUZZ_PATH=... pnpm test:fuzz` replays it

```ts
// tests/fuzz/generator-lab-steps.fuzz.test.ts
it("step ids come from the step file name, not the course's parent directories", () => {
```

---

## The middle: generator, BDD, contract (C, D, H)

- `tests/generator/`: runs the candidate generator over a committed corpus and diffs it; every intended
  difference needs a claim in `claims.yaml`
- `tests/bdd/`: 85 feature files and 441 Rules; each Rule is one EARS sentence, its scenarios the proof
- `tests/contract/`: export names of the JSR packages (`gen-lib-api.test.ts`), and Zod schemas for
  Supabase and realtime shapes

Counts from the clone at `2b96b69`: `pnpm test:ears:audit` prints
`OK: 85 feature file(s), 441 Rule(s), 0 new violation(s)`.

---

## The top: e2e-stack (G, F)

- Named journeys against the **built container images**, with a fixture course server
- `journeys/negative.journey.spec.ts` holds `test.fail()` journeys: proof that a journey can fail
- No retries. "A journey that needs one is a finding."

The reader's browser Rules (`features/ui/`) are proved by Playwright tests in `apps/reader/tests/e2e`,
one per scenario, tagged with the Rule id.

---

## The runway: checks over the repository (A, J to O)

| Folder | Fails when |
| --- | --- |
| `architecture/` | an import goes up a layer; manifests drift; unused files appear |
| `conformance/` | an env var or manifest breaks the platform contract (tier J) |
| `observability/` | a log line leaves the schema |
| `security/` | a mutating route is not listed with who may call it |
| `completeness/` | a translation, theme token or relative link is missing |
| `suite-health/` | `.only`, an unquarantined skip, a test with no assertion, an unbound feature |

`pnpm test:runway` runs seven of them together.

---

## Verify, do not trust the map

Checked against the clone:

- `tests/validate-corpus/` and `tests/fixtures/` exist but have no row in the map. The first holds real
  courses for `pnpm check:validate-corpus`; the second holds inputs other suites read.
- The map says Stryker runs over "twelve modules"; `stryker.config.json` lists fourteen
  (`course-builder.ts` and `resource-builder.ts` were added).

A map is L2. It drifts. Rung 5 makes its own map a test: `tests/architecture/test-tiers.test.ts`.

---

## Ratchets

Checks that found problems on day one hold them in a baseline beside the suite:
`architecture/known-violations.txt`, `suite-health/known-findings.txt`, ...

- A new problem fails
- A baseline line that no longer occurs also fails
- So a baseline can only shrink

Rung 5 starts clean, so it has no baselines: every finding fails.

---

## Rung 5's pyramid

```
tests/
  unit/          gen, lite, cli            (rung 3's tests, now on Vitest)
  bdd/           features + steps          12 requirements, 19 scenarios
  contract/      FileSystemPort, package API
  conformance/   upstream's tutors.json, one golden page
  fuzz/          5 property suites, each with a "can fail" fixture
  architecture/  rung 3's rules + test tiers
  suite-health/  closure check, EARS audit, no .only/.skip
  mutation/      the floor check
  assignment/    skipped until LADDER_ASSIGNMENT=1
```

One command still runs it all: `pnpm test`.

---

## The same names, a different scale

| Rung 5 | Upstream | Difference |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `tests/conformance/` | `tests/generator/` | here "conformance" means the generator against an oracle; upstream's `conformance/` is the platform |
| `tests/fuzz/` | `tests/fuzz/` | in the gate here; its own config upstream |
| `tests/mutation/` | `tests/mutation/` | floors only; Stryker runs outside the gate in both |
| none | `e2e-stack/`, `performance/`, ... | no browser and no server on this rung |

---

## Why switch to Vitest

- Rung 3 used `node --test`
- Stryker has no `node:test` runner. Its `command` runner reruns everything per mutant, with no per-test
  results
- Upstream uses Vitest, Stryker's vitest runner and vitest-cucumber
- So rung 5 switched. Rung 3's tests kept `node:assert` and changed one import line.

---

## Take away

- Each tier answers one question: does this function work, does this feature meet its rule, does this
  double tell the truth, does the output match the oracle, would the suite notice a change?
- Read `tests/TESTING.md` upstream, then check it against the folders.
- Next: how a requirement becomes something a test can check.
